  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nA cold front and upper trough will deliver a combination of lower\ntemperatures, increasing west to northwest winds, and continued \nshowers and thunderstorms through this evening. SPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk for the east, and a small area of slight risk over\nWibaux county, where severe wind gusts are a concern. These \nlocations will see the warmest daytime temperatures and more \nunstable atmospheric conditions. Overall, HREF shows timing as \nearly as 18Z for the wind threat emerging, with activity evolving \neast by evening hours. A few of the storms may produce locally \nheavy rainfall as well. Long, narrow tracks of updraft helicity \ngreater than 75 m^2/s^2 present the possibility of isolated \nsupercells clipping our CWA.\n\nThe Lake Wind Advisory for Fort Peck Lake remains in effect until\n6 PM MDT. Windy conditions will relax during the evening but ramp\nback up tomorrow. That said, an additional Lake Wind Advisory has\nbeen issued for tomorrow from 6 AM to 6 PM MDT. The strongest \nwinds will be in Sheridan, Daniels, and eastern Roosevelt county,\nfor a brief period, gusting near headline criteria.\n\nUpper ridging will then guide warmer and drier conditions to NE\nMontana for Tuesday. The ridge axis looks to slide east late in\nthe week with diffluent southwest upper flow becoming established\nover the Northern High Plains. In short, the next round of\nthunderstorms may get underway as early as Wednesday evening.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nAdjusted Pops using HiRes ARW for near term collaboration and \nconsistency. Following an increase in PoPs, adjusted PoT to match\nthe SVR threat for this afternoon. Upped winds using 33% NBM90 for\ntoday, and 50/50 NBM/NBM90 for winds and wind gusts Monday. \n\nConfidence is also increasing on more active weather in the form \nof thunderstorms by midweek as the ridge translates downstream, \nsupported by dynamic lift and increased instability.",
